UNIT vector_set_sha
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs, export vectorSetSha — the oracle hash every oracle file's canonicalSha256
  and every primary's ORACLE CANONICAL-SHA256 declares; SIR_SCHEMA §10 extension states the same rule ("SHA-256 of the
  canonical string of {frozen: [{args, expected}…], heldout: […]}")
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG vector_set_sha(oracle: json) -> text
ENVELOPE an oracle file's json: a map whose "frozen" and "heldout" are lists of case maps, each case holding "args" and
  "expected" as CANONICAL TREES (text, bool, list, map[text, tree]; numbers and none appear only inside typed-value maps
  such as {"t":"f64","bits":..}, {"t":"i64","v":".."}, {"t":"none"}). Every json value is IN the envelope: one that is
  not such an oracle THROWS (R1, R2, R4, R5), and that throw is part of the contract. The language-neutral oracle carries
  the oracle value nested up to the codec's 100-value cap (the oracle is depth 0, so args and expected carry at most 97
  levels below them); the R5 boundary lies deeper, so its vectors are a js-only extension.
DEPENDS-ON canonical_string (selfhost unit, re-derived; its contract is summarized in R4 so this file stands alone)
DESCRIPTION The fingerprint of an oracle's vector set: project the oracle down to the two ordered case lists, keep only
  each case's args and expected, render that projection as its canonical string and hash it. Two oracles get the same
  hash exactly when their projections render identically, so case names, extra case keys, extra top-level keys, f64
  display text and throw messages never move the hash, while case order, the frozen/heldout split, duplicates, the bit
  pattern of every f64 and every other byte of args and expected always do.
BEHAVIOR
R1 The oracle must be a map holding key "frozen" and key "heldout", each bound to a list; otherwise -> throws. That
  covers an oracle that is not a map (list, text, f64, bool, none), a missing key, and a key bound to anything but a
  list (none, a map, text, empty text included). Every other top-level key is ignored and never examined.
R2 Every case, in both lists, must be a map; otherwise -> throws (none, text, a list, an f64). From a case only the
  values under "args" and "expected" are taken, and both keys must be present (a missing one -> throws); every other key
  of the case ("name", "note", anything) is ignored and never examined.
R3 The PROJECTION is the map with exactly two entries: "frozen" -> the list of {"args": A, "expected": E} built from the
  frozen cases in their order, and "heldout" -> the same from the heldout cases. Order is preserved, duplicate cases
  are kept, nothing is sorted or de-duplicated, and an empty list stays empty.
R4 CANON is canonical_string(PROJECTION) (unit canonical_string); a throw there is a throw here. Summary of that
  contract: text renders as a JSON string literal (quotation mark, reverse solidus, backspace, form feed, line feed,
  carriage return and tab as their two-character short escapes; every other control 0x00..0x1F as the six-character
  unicode escape with LOWER-case hex; everything else raw, non-ASCII included); bool as true/false; a list as
  [e1,e2,..]; a map as {"k":v,..} with keys sorted ascending by 16-bit code units; no whitespace anywhere outside text.
  A map whose "t" is exactly the text "f64" renders as {"bits":B,"t":"f64"} and requires "bits" to be text of exactly
  16 characters from 0-9 and lower-case a-f (else throws); a map whose "t" is exactly "throw" renders as {"t":"throw"};
  in both the other entries are dropped unexamined. A raw f64 or a none reached by the rendering throws. Hence CANON
  always begins {"frozen":[ and each case renders as {"args":A,"expected":E}. canonical_string's depth cap is R5. CITED ANCHOR SIR_SCHEMA §10
R5 NESTING CAP (canonical_string's depth rule, seen through the projection). Depth is counted in the PROJECTION: the
  projection itself is depth 0 and every value directly inside a list or a map (a map's values; keys are never counted) is
  one deeper than its container, so the frozen and heldout lists are depth 1, each case map depth 2, and every case's args
  and expected depth 3. The rendering throws as soon as it reaches a value deeper than 416 (= 4 × 100 + 16, from the
  codec's 100-value cap). Only REACHED values count: the dropped entries of an f64 or throw node are never measured, while
  the texts such a node does render ("t" and "bits" of an f64, "t" of a throw) sit one level below the node. Measured from
  args or expected as depth 0: a reached value at depth 413 renders, one at 414 or deeper -> throws; an f64 or throw node
  renders at depth 412 at most. Nesting of any depth past the cap is this throw. A wide but shallow tree never throws for
  its size. CITED FIDELITY package
R6 The result is SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) of CANON's bytes in the 8-bit Unicode encoding (each scalar value as 1 to 4
  bytes, §10's cu utf8 form), written as 64 LOWER-case hexadecimal digits. CITED FIDELITY package
R7 Whether the oracle throws is the observation; the message is a display hint and is not part of the contract. When
  several causes are present, any of them may be the one reported.
INVARIANT renaming cases, adding or removing ignored keys, rewriting an f64's display "v" with the same "bits", or
  rewriting a throw's "msg" never changes the result.
INVARIANT the result depends only on the projection's values, never on the order in which any map's keys were written.
INVARIANT moving a case between frozen and heldout, or swapping two unequal cases, changes CANON and so the result.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN js widening (no oracle vector; the codec cannot carry them): an absent value under "args" or "expected"
  throws like a missing key; a frozen or heldout list with holes throws; text holding unpaired surrogates renders them
  as six-character unicode escapes with lower-case hex, so CANON stays ASCII-safe for them; a map whose prototype is
  absent is a map like any other. A "__proto__" key is outside the oracle. Nesting past R5's cap is IN the contract and
  is a throw at every depth beyond it (the cap is checked on entry to each value, so the rendering never descends past 417).
ORACLE value -> oracles/vector_set_sha.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 7510cd1d2064be9a61f8d9b52ad40285424ec83fe35dda8e1b22081cd63d1b70
SCOPE IN: R1-R7 over every json value. OUT: canonical_string's own vector set (a separate unit, depended on here), the
  oracle file's other fields, and the comparison of the result against a declared hash (callers, not this unit).
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 (rederive, own code), cli/sir/canon.mjs, export vectorSetSha, driven through
  selfhost/seams/vector_set_sha.mjs; oracle stamped by selfhost/stamp.mjs on the default host stack from the pinned
  authority snapshot (24 frozen + 48 held-out, seed 1). Round 1 cut 2026-10-26; round 4 re-cut 2026-10-27 for the nesting
  cap (FINDINGS #66); round 5 re-cut 2026-10-27 for the 416 raw cap (FINDINGS #78).
